{UTF-8}
Project Initiation Rate=IF THEN ELSE(
		MAX( 0, BESS Capacity under Construction) + Operational BESS Capacity >= REFERENCE BESS CAPACITY\
		, 
		0, 
		MAX INITIATION RATE * "INVESTMENT LOOKUP RESPONSE (FLAT)"(Revenue to Cost Ratio)
		)
	~	MW/Year
	~		|

Procurement Cost=IF THEN ELSE(
	Conservative Revenue Floor for 1 Battery > Enveloped Annual Revenue for 1 Battery, 
	(Conservative Revenue Floor for 1 Battery - Enveloped Annual Revenue for 1 Battery) \
		* (Operational BESS Capacity / REFERENCE BATTERY SIZE), 
	0)
	~	Euro/Year
	~	Annual net fiscal cost to EirGrid. Positive = government is losing money \
		paying out revenue floor. Negative = government receiving revenue because \
		annual arbitrage revenue is above revenue floor.
	|

Curtailment Reduction Rate=MIN (	"Curtailment (Without BESS)", 
		Operational BESS Capacity * CHARGING DURATION*CYCLES PER DAY * 365 * CURTAILMENT CAPTURE RATE
	)
	~	MWh/Year
	~	Your 4-hour, 100 MW battery has 400 MWh of capacity. You’ve already set CYCLES PER \
		DAY = 0.8. What that means in practice: each day, the battery absorbs 400 \
		MWh × 0.8 = 320 MWh from the grid during charging.
		Now, the whole point of the LDES service is to charge during curtailment events \
		(that’s what “re-dispatch” means in EirGrid’s proposal). So \
		charging = absorbing curtailment. Per year, that’s 320 MWh × 365 = \
		116,800 MWh = 116.8 GWh per 100 MW battery.
		
		at 40% restriction, only 40% of the battery's physical capacity is \
		directed by the TSO toward curtailment absorption. The rest is for free \
		arbitrage.
	|

"INVESTMENT LOOKUP RESPONSE (FLAT)"([(0,0)-(2.6,1)],
	(0,0),
	(0.2,0.002),
	(0.4,0.007),
	(0.6,0.018),
	(0.8,0.039),
	(1,0.08),
	(1.2,0.151),
	(1.4,0.256),
	(1.6,0.391),
	(1.8,0.546),
	(2,0.711),
	(2.2,0.861),
	(2.4,0.961),
	(2.6,1))
	~	Dimensionless
	~	How investors translate risk-adjusted return into new project pipeline as \
		a fraction of Max Pipeline Rate. Below 1.0 (break-even) nothing gets \
		built. Ramps up above, saturates at high returns.
	|

"INVESTMENT LOOKUP RESPONSE (STEEP)"([(0,0)-(2.6,1)],
	(0,0),
	(0.2,0.001),
	(0.4,0.004),
	(0.6,0.012),
	(0.8,0.03),
	(1,0.08),
	(1.2,0.23),
	(1.4,0.64),
	(1.6,0.89),
	(1.8,0.96),
	(2,0.98),
	(2.2,0.991),
	(2.4,0.997),
	(2.6,1))
	~	Dimensionless
	~	How investors translate risk-adjusted return into new project pipeline as \
		a fraction of Max Pipeline Rate. Below 1.0 (break-even) nothing gets \
		built. Ramps up above, saturates at high returns.
	|

Average Buying Price=( MIN BUYING PRICE + (MAX BUYING PRICE - MIN BUYING PRICE) * (1 \
		- Effective Curtailment/ "Curtailment (Without BESS)") ) * ( "Curtailment (Without BESS)"\
		 / BASE CURTAILMENT DATA ) ^ CURTAILMENT PRICE ELASTICITY
	~	Euro/MWh
	~	The average price the battery charges at. The average buying price starts \
		at its floor, then climbs toward its ceiling depending on how much \
		curtailment has been absorbed.
	|

"Revenue to Cost Ratio (Without Revenue Floor)"=Potential Annual Arbitrage Revenue for 1 Battery\
		/Expected Annual Costs
	~	Dimensionless
	~		|

CURTAILMENT PRICE ELASTICITY=- 0.0626
	~	Dimensionless
	~		|

"ENVELOPE RESTRICTION (SENSITIVITY)"=0.2
	~	Dimensionless
	~	When ENVELOPE RESTRICTION (SENSITIVITY) = 0.4, it feeds into:
		Potential Annual Arbitrage Revenue for 1 Battery * (1 - 0.4)
		
		So the battery only earns 60% of its unconstrained arbitrage revenue. It \
		means the TSO reduced the earnings potential by 40%
	|

Curtailment Reduction Percentage=Curtailment Reduction Rate/ "Curtailment (Without BESS)"
	~	Percentage
	~		|

Project Completion Rate=BESS Capacity under Construction/Construction Time
	~	MW/Year
	~	Projects exit construction after Construction Time years.
	|

Conservative Revenue Floor for 1 Battery="LCOS (STARTING POINT)"*ANNUAL STORAGE OUTPUT\
		*"REVENUE FLOOR FACTOR (SENSITIVITY)"
	~	Euro/Year
	~		|

CURTAILMENT CAPTURE RATE=0.75
	~	Dimensionless
	~		|

MIN BUYING PRICE=81.05
	~	Euro/MWh
	~	Min Buying Price is the average of the four cheapest hours each day across 2023 - \
		2025, reflecting the optimal charging strategy of a rational 4-hour \
		battery operator.
		
		
		Where to get the data:
		- Hourly day ahead prices (select the cheapest 4 hours everyday and \
		average it)
	|

"REVENUE FLOOR FACTOR (SENSITIVITY)"=0.7
	~	Dimensionless
	~	SCENARIO INPUT. 0 = no floor, 1 = full cost recovery.
	|

CURTAILMENT ANNUAL GROWTH RATE=0.28
	~	Dimensionless
	~	Phase 1 - 80% renewable reached by 2030 - look at ECP 2.4 Scenario FG ECP 3.1 \
		Offshore. 
		Phase 2 - slow growth at 2% until 2039
	|

Effective Curtailment=MAX(0, "Curtailment (Without BESS)"- Curtailment Reduction Rate\
		)
	~	MWh/Year
	~	Annual curtailment remaining after LDES absorbs what it can. This is the \
		annual feedback signal to AVERAGE BUYING PRICE. Bounded at zero.
	|

"Curtailment (Without BESS)"=BASE CURTAILMENT DATA * (1 + CURTAILMENT ANNUAL GROWTH RATE\
		) ^ (Time - 2027)
	~	MWh/Year
	~		|

BESS Capacity under Construction= INTEG (Project Initiation Rate-Project Completion Rate\
		,
		0)
	~	MW
	~		|

"Levelised Cost of Storage (LCOS)"="LCOS (STARTING POINT)" * (1 - 0.02736) ^ (Time - \
		2027)
	~	Euro/MWh
	~	https://docs.nrel.gov/docs/fy25osti/93281.pdf
		Table 2 - Appendix (page 16)
		
		Low scenario 
		2027 - USD 243
		2037 - USD 142
		
		CAGR =  (142/243)^(1/10) - 1 = -5.2% per year
		
		Final value = - 0.052
		
		
		
		Lazard Levelized Cost of Storage—Key Assumptions, page 43
		
		Charging Cost Escalator - 1.97%
		
		Final value = 0.02
	|

"LCOS (STARTING POINT)"=130
	~	Euro/MWh
	~	Estimates taken from benchmark from Ember Report on How cheap are batteries.
		
		https://ember-energy.org/app/uploads/2025/12/How-cheap-is-battery-storage.pdf
		
		page 13
		
		
		65
	|

REFERENCE BATTERY SIZE=100
	~	MW
	~	Power capacity of the reference battery unit used for per-project
		revenue and cost calculations.
		One reference battery = 100 MW / 400 MWh (4-hour duration).
		Used as denominator in Procurement Cost to correctly scale
		from per-battery costs to whole-fleet costs.
		Do NOT confuse with REFERENCE BESS CAPACITY (500 MW).
	|

Expected Annual Costs="Levelised Cost of Storage (LCOS)" * ANNUAL STORAGE OUTPUT
	~	Euro/Year
	~		|

Expected Annual Revenue for 1 Battery=MAX(Revenue Floor for 1 Battery, Enveloped Annual Revenue for 1 Battery\
		)
	~	Euro/Year
	~		|

Revenue Floor for 1 Battery="Levelised Cost of Storage (LCOS)" * ANNUAL STORAGE OUTPUT\
		 * "REVENUE FLOOR FACTOR (SENSITIVITY)"
	~	Euro/Year
	~	Guaranteed revenue from the revenue floor mechanism.
	|

Enveloped Annual Revenue for 1 Battery=Potential Annual Arbitrage Revenue for 1 Battery\
		 * (1 - "ENVELOPE RESTRICTION (SENSITIVITY)")
	~	Euro/Year
	~		|

Revenue to Cost Ratio=Expected Annual Revenue for 1 Battery/Expected Annual Costs
	~	Dimensionless
	~	Revenue-to-cost ratio. 1.0 = break-even; above 1 is profitable; below 1 is \
		loss-making.
	|

Cumulative Avoided Curtailment= INTEG (Curtailment Reduction Rate,
		0)
	~	MWh
	~		|

Cumulative Procurement Mechanism Cost= INTEG (Procurement Cost,
		0)
	~	Euro
	~	Total of the net fiscal cost of the procurement mechanism to EirGrid from \
		the start of the simulation. Positive = net cost. Negative = net revenue \
		to EirGrid.
	|

Abitrage Spread=Average Selling Price-Average Buying Price
	~	Euro/MWh
	~	Difference between Average Selling Price and Average Buying Price.
	|

Potential Annual Arbitrage Revenue for 1 Battery=ENERGY CAPACITY * CYCLES PER DAY * 365\
		 * Abitrage Spread
	~	Euro/Year
	~	Annual arbitrage earnings per 100 MW / 400 MWh project (before revenue \
		floor).
	|

MAX INITIATION RATE=500
	~	MW/Year
	~	The maximum limit on new capacity the industry can initiate per year.
	|

MAX SELLING PRICE=162.98
	~	Euro/MWh
	~	Max Selling Price is the price a battery operator receives when selling electricity \
		during peak demand periods — before any cannibalisation from other BESS \
		occurs. It’s the best case selling price, representing normal peak \
		market conditions.
		
		Where to get data:
		- hourly day ahead price (average of the 4 most expensive hours each day)
	|

Average Selling Price=MAX(MIN SELLING PRICE, MAX SELLING PRICE - (MAX SELLING PRICE -\
		 MIN SELLING PRICE) * Operational BESS Capacity/REFERENCE BESS CAPACITY)
	~	Euro/MWh
	~	The average selling price is the price a battery operator receives when discharging \
		electricity into the grid, and it falls as more BESS capacity is built.
		
		It starts at its MAX SELLING PRICE — when no BESS exists and peak prices \
		are undisturbed. As more batteries come online and all discharge during \
		the same peak windows, they flood that period with supply and push prices \
		down. The more BESS that gets built, the lower the selling price drops. \
		But it can never fall below the MIN SELLING PRICE no matter how much \
		capacity is built.
	|

MIN SELLING PRICE=112.07
	~	Euro/MWh
	~	It’s the price your battery receives when the market is at its worst for selling \
		— when lots of BESS are all discharging at the same time and pushing \
		peak prices down.
		
		Min Selling Price is fundamentally a future state that doesn’t exist in your \
		historical data. It represents a market condition — 500 MW of BESS all \
		discharging simultaneously — that hasn’t happened in Ireland yet. No \
		amount of clever filtering of your existing dataset can truly capture that.
		
		https://www.pv-magazine.de/2023/07/11/preiseffekte-durch-den-ausbau-von-batteriespeic\
		hern-teil-3-arbitrage-in-der-day-ahead-auktion/
		says that the base price pushes up from 115 to 120 with 100MW of battery.
		
		Germany
		The German market is huge. 100 MW of battery is a tiny drop in a very large ocean. \
		It represents 0.5% of total market volume. Yet even that tiny share caused \
		a 7.8% drop in peak prices. That’s already surprisingly large.
		Ireland
		Ireland’s market is much smaller. 500 MW of battery is a much bigger drop in a \
		much smaller ocean. So the price impact will be proportionally larger.
		
		Steps to Carry Out:
		1. Calculate Ireland’s market share Get the average hourly demand in MWh for \
		2023-2025
		Ireland’s market share = 100 / average hourly Irish demand × 100
		2. Compare Ireland’s market share of 100 MW to Germany’s Germany: 100 MW / \
		20,000 MWh = 0.5% market share → caused 7.8% (EUR 16) decrease in \
		selling price (discharge) Ireland: 100 MW / ?? MWh = ?? market share \
		→ caused ??% decrease in selling price (discharge)
		Concentration ratio = Ireland’s market share / Germany’s market share (0.5%)
		This tells you how many times more concentrated 100 MW is in Ireland versus Germany.
		3. Compare Ireland’s mid merit share to Germany’s Germany has much higher mid \
		merit generation capacity, which gives BESS more room to compress prices \
		because BESS can displace expensive gas plants easier and move it down to \
		a mid merit plant.
		Mid merit ratio = Ireland’s mid merit share / Germany’s mid merit share
		4. Get the Adjusted Cannibalisation Rate for 100 MW of BESS for Ireland
		Adjusted cannibalisation = 7.8% * Mid merit ratio * Concentration ratio
		5. Scale for 500 MW Multiply each result by 5 since I have 500 MW not 100 MW.
		6. Calculate Min Selling Price
		Min Selling Price = Max Selling Price (162.98) - scaled €/MWh reduction
	|

MAX BUYING PRICE=103.76
	~	Euro/MWh
	~	The price your battery charges at when all curtailment has been absorbed \
		and those cheap surplus windows no longer exist. Essentially just the \
		normal average wholesale electricity price in Ireland during off-peak \
		hours — what you’d pay when there’s no surplus pushing prices down.
	|

CHARGING DURATION=4
	~	Hours/Cycle
	~	Battery duration at rated power. Fixed at 4 hours for this thesis.
	|

REFERENCE BESS CAPACITY=500
	~	MW
	~	The total amount of BESS you expect to be built by the end of your \
		simulation. It’s the “fully built out” scenario — the point at \
		which cannibalisation is at its maximum.
	|

ANNUAL STORAGE OUTPUT=105120
	~	MWh/Year
	~	MWh delivered annually by a 100 MW / 400 MWh battery cycling at 0.9/day, 350 \
		days/year.
		
		The Ember calculator derived your LCOS of $130/MWh using utilisation = 0.8 \
		and efficiency = 0.9, which gives Year-1 energy of 262.8 MWh per 1 MWh of \
		storage capacity (0.8 × 0.9 × 365 = 262.8). Scaled to your 400 MWh \
		battery, that implies 105,120 MWh/yr of annual output.
	|

BASE CURTAILMENT DATA=258000
	~	MWh/Year
	~	Constraints represent dispatch down applied to manage localised congestion on the \
		grid, whereby variable generator output is constrained to stay within \
		overload limits of the transmission lines. This is applied at a nodal \
		level.
		
		Ireland Solar Constraints in 2024 - 32,006 MWh (4.3% of Total Available Energy) 
		EirGrid Annual Renewable Energy Constraint and Curtailment Report 2024, p23 
		https://cms.eirgrid.ie/sites/default/files/publications/Annual-Renewable-Constraint-a\
		nd-Curtailment-Report-2024-V1.0.pdf 
		
		Ireland Wind Curtailment in 2024 - 627,440 MWh (5.0% of Total Available Energy)
		EirGrid Annual Renewable Energy Constraint and Curtailment Report 2024, p20
		
		Total Constraints = 659,446 MWh
	|

Retirement Rate=0
	~	MW/Year
	~	Set to zero: LDES assets have 20+ year lives, so no retirements in the \
		2027-2032 horizon.
	|

Construction Time=2
	~	Year
	~	Average time from contract signing to operation for battery projects.
	|

CYCLES PER DAY=0.8
	~	Dimensionless
	~	1 cycle means the battery charges for 4 hours and discharges for 4 hours per day. \
		Set to 0.9 because of efficiency issues. This is used by EirGrid \
		https://cms.eirgrid.ie/sites/default/files/publications/ECP-2.4-Solar-and-W\
		ind-Constraints-Report-Assumptions-and-Methodology-v1.1.pdf
		
		See p 19
	|

ENERGY CAPACITY=400 * 0.9
	~	MWh
	~	The battrery size for a 4 hour battery.
	|

